      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "// HARDCODE YOUR TECH STACK HERE\nconst myTechStack = \"Windows Server 2022, Python 3.9, Apache Tomcat, AWS EC2\";\n\nreturn items.map(item => {\n  const fields = item.json.fields || {};\n\n  // FIX: Fall back through all available text fields so Groq always has context\n  const title = fields['Title'] || item.json['Title'] || 'Untitled';\n  const affectedSoftware = fields['Affected Software'] || item.json['Affected Software'] || '';\n  const summary = fields['Summary'] || item.json['Summary'] || '';\n  const rawDescription = fields['Raw Description'] || item.json['Raw Description'] || '';\n  const rawContent = fields['Raw Content'] || item.json['Raw Content'] || '';\n\n  // Use the richest available text, in priority order\n  const threatText = summary || rawDescription || rawContent || 'No description available.';\n\n  const prompt = `You are an expert cybersecurity analyst. Evaluate a cyber threat against this specific technology stack: ${myTechStack}\n\nThreat Title: ${title}\nAffected Software: ${affectedSoftware || 'Not specified'}\nThreat Details: ${threatText}\n\nEvaluate the overlap between this threat and the tech stack. Consider:\n- Does the affected software match anything in the tech stack?\n- Does the threat target platforms or services used in this stack?\n- Even if not an exact match, is this threat category relevant (e.g. Apache vulnerabilities for a Tomcat shop)?\n\nOutput strictly in JSON format with one key:\n- \"score\": A number from 0 to 100 representing how relevant this threat is to the tech stack. Use 0 only if there is truly zero relevance.\n\nDo not include any markdown formatting, backticks, or extra text. Just the JSON object.`;\n\n  return {\n    json: {\n      recordId: item.json.id,\n      prompt: prompt\n    }\n  };\n});"
      },
